ence,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2049f5f7-1d2c-5379-b695-8528aba89f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345636} {:answer/id #uuid "aacbc7a0-b134-5a75-a87d-fd53948b0296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346843} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9db866-8720-598b-af83-114dd5773144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346978} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80fc1e-6eab-5ddb-9563-26cba2c2d2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347298} {:answer/id #uuid "f22c335d-cda9-5ae9-995c-087b8ca8dae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aacbc7a0-b134-5a75-a87d-fd53948b0296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346843} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5d2966-8562-5e0a-b5f1-e9381bdd7052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ac549bb-41f9-5150-895e-d407a77f8e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-78131" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aeaace-3dee-5861-928d-6a23090d00f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345366} {:answer/id #uuid "01c007c5-b958-5f05-bcdb-d16eb849a4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345367} {:answer/id #uuid "02100b32-fbcc-582d-903e-bf23af1e8c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345368} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6767c-d2f1-526e-934e-fbbf34e09777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347068} {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 1759