db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396164} {:answer/id #uuid "610851d8-2303-56fb-9fc0-aefe88bab851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396165} {:answer/id #uuid "25f771aa-b7d7-5d86-a28a-ca24f26dc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396166} {:answer/id #uuid "151edfbf-fc56-54ae-9bf2-3f2f8dc0b2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396167} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb96d3c-a223-5810-9d7d-8755c8adf510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f771aa-b7d7-5d86-a28a-ca24f26dc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396166} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f553809-f578-5b97-b742-22d7fa6d05fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ba95bcc-83a0-57ed-9254-81c22af4a048" :question/math-academy-id "q-7835" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $K$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $L$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- $M$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $4$ rows and $4$ columns, so it is a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, only $L$ and $M$ are square matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d6c920-989f-5ce5-a8d5-c2abc399051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345915} {:answer/id #uuid "5b65b5d5-07d7-5975-9ee4-291d6b399353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346130} {:answer/id #uuid "9e973f9e-81b5-5854-88b0-98066c9a699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346735} {:answer/id #uuid "b2220b9a-c972-5a58-a399-44ce9b253549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346905} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfc5434-346a-59f7-b3db-0e599e3994ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d6c920-989f-5ce5-a8d5-c2abc399051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345915} :answer-field/id #uuid "13539e3e-82b2-55bb-b37b-e5e32ff876f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8280a162-8b28-5252-92be-7c19e3ebbf0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78379" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y=12?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y & =12 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-12y-3{x}^{2}+12x & =12 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-6y]-3[{x}^{2}-4x] & =12 \\\\ 2[(y-3)^{2}-9]-3[(x-2)^{2}-4] & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-18-3(x-2)^{2}+12 & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =12+18-12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(2,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e970fe-cff8-50e3-9b94-67ad97b03da7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d718e860-d9b8-5907-8e53-05046524911e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 14 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393865} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2da8db-419b-5fc2-a2f6-c5d25d2ee8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393866} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c7ef1-9d5b-59ed-9bfc-b71aaba92cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393867} {:answer/id #uuid "41965806-1f4e-5183-9b81-5ddeff998ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393868} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1e5569-d116-55e7-87e8-e2b89abc51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1e5569-d116-55e7-87e8-e2b89abc51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393869} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d67059-f6d7-5521-9126-3196f2ed6789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88934d15-ae66-5c80-a236-6a63bba3d33b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78497" :question/problem "If $F=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${F}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${F}^{2},$ we multiply $F$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{2} & =F\\cdot F \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5fd665-92c8-5de9-a962-94e1f5665e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393879} {:answer/id #uuid "b49b5346-149e-5688-b4e3-abd91ac82b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393880} {:answer/id #uuid "dde44d62-1c85-5ae3-b2b1-d15a071a6e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393881} {:answer/id #uuid "86f61021-d77c-5497-9ea7-8d2d27863b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393882} {:answer/id #uuid "6286448b-0a3f-5139-a73d-05652685e000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6286448b-0a3f-5139-a73d-05652685e000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393883} :answer-field/id #uuid "4352c172-b7da-52e6-b834-2727aa018a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c98c808b-049b-59b0-8d36-61df2339933c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78549" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+9z=0 \\\\ y+6z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-3,z=1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-3)+1=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d740f60-2523-5646-9a92-3c0d339e8fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393886} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f32d4d-f862-5218-b3d1-078edcdfe213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393887} {:answer/id #uuid "12eac5a8-05a9-5828-8db5-751459d1b149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393888} {:answer/id #uuid "5af9cd45-f4f1-5692-8de2-840ab3c6c7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393889} {:answer/id #uuid "44860cb9-5129-5c07-80fe-7caff5e9aed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f32d4d-f862-5218-b3d1-078edcdfe213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393887} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d59287-5dfe-56f3-93e2-54429b0c04b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07f12953-92a4-55d6-afcb-39fb0f43a712" :question/math-academy-id "q-78552" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-3y+2z=-4 \\\\ -4x+4y-3z=7 \\\\ x-y+z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=-1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+2+(-1)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} {:answer/id #uuid "6898f8cf-ef60-599d-b703-aeb35e14e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403950} {:answer/id #uuid "0329ed6f-dee9-5205-b238-18984ed73972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403951} {:answer/id #uuid "a15466de-3c65-5970-8e36-5b25e21ca209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${D}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403952} {:answer/id #uuid "daabc2d4-f4ce-50f8-9f41-a9e7585ac6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf7d7a2-cf8f-5d79-a5a3-2c7fb6abb055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "863bd08e-01cd-5a69-b3ce-bacc176f8b4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78559" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,z=c.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $z$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $c$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & =c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and right to left, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,z=c$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & ={D}^{-1}c \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f916d57-a48d-5f42-a1e6-2cc8f554dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} {:answer/id #uuid "a2636cd7-9981-5e94-8ce6-ffc7ac605d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403957} {:answer/id #uuid "cd887cdb-1f42-5d46-bc3e-089ab176ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403958} {:answer/id #uuid "e14838e1-6770-514b-a8d3-264fece5dc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403959} {:answer/id #uuid "45769dbd-ae3d-5976-b913-0ac9c72c7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f916d57-a48d-5f42-a1e6-2cc8f554dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} :answer-field/id #uuid "96efe5ef-8066-599a-a9a3-934df32a5fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf8e0a5-c1dd-5243-9116-de59a516e7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-78561" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ \\boxed{\\phantom{D^{-1}}}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,\\boxed{\\phantom{q}} \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0703d799-0111-55e4-99f9-70c3eb59a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345414} {:answer/id #uuid "40758a02-7b8c-5d72-ab58-a8d20d28afdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345905} {:answer/id #uuid "63992de9-ec6d-51ab-a206-cde915c80422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346218} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb31ca7-c07d-505f-bbbb-56822f2e4ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346736} {:answer/id #uuid "f59b0784-1475-5ee1-bbd4-044ae1258144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0703d799-0111-55e4-99f9-70c3eb59a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345414} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2ac7a2-8856-589e-8887-844636305c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "209d3fb4-3f5a-5f61-958c-85386d790787" :question/math-academy-id "q-78871" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y=-47?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x-{y}^{2}-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]-[{y}^{2}+2y] & =-47 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]-[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50-(y+1)^{2}+1 & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =-47+50-1 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(5,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ad9106-6449-5425-9ea8-8c4d982508a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345373} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc42bc-9e4d-55b0-9f61-b8cbcec2623e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345960} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe63e-62d3-5e7b-87ef-17e62dfe7c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346988} {:answer/id #uuid "e4746b2e-d4c3-51e0-b47c-2a5f28dfd1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbb2f12-0ff8-5ad2-898f-75f1bc5be3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "858cfde7-5900-59e1-9b39-5d78acc22281" :question/math-academy-id "q-78950" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b139ee-f463-5a47-9c6a-a41512770e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345503} {:answer/id #uuid "19031ec8-e1ff-542a-a61e-a82e8669c57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345572} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3696a9-c1f5-5688-a685-20e48aa47576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345625} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a736b8-6f9b-5cac-8168-604e09548620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347219} {:answer/id #uuid "ee140afe-fb06-5d83-b607-8a7268797ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee140afe-fb06-5d83-b607-8a7268797ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347453} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce54c950-38e1-57f1-9dbf-c1bfdc34311a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57251548-96ca-57aa-b00f-0da810dd66ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-78951" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{csc}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbf0b70-eb73-5737-870e-2b2a733a604d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345593} {:answer/id #uuid "22ea9738-c711-5104-a068-657f18af36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345664} {:answer/id #uuid "58320d87-d5f1-5831-b7ba-1fc8e51312dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346104} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} {:answer/id #uuid "de267612-3488-5cfc-bf84-8951e79d0127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb795487-a850-5ed5-84bc-cdf04a7dfdc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2921b965-ad16-58ab-8156-5829abb484a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78954" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2,\\,y={t}^{7}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7{t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7{t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2)={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7{t}^{2})}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14t}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=2}=\\frac{14}{(2)^{3}}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b374944-d47a-526e-b651-a9bdd179683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The locus has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405189} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc41c5f-d7b6-5d05-8b47-2e4e472399bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405190} {:answer/id #uuid "d443e6bd-6dee-5d3a-b69a-1e6b48338e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1-\\sqrt{11},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{11},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405191} {:answer/id #uuid "73af1a99-304f-5788-9f9f-7c5af517eb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405192} {:answer/id #uuid "eec08ca2-05c0-5cfd-a7e9-7540222dfb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{11},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b374944-d47a-526e-b651-a9bdd179683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The locus has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405189} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56e38c9-b83e-56e9-bf4c-ec09e761bb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f43a31ca-f582-5ca4-a303-c0af1e9c11d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79044" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the real locus ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the locus and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, the locus has no $x$-intercepts."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37348c9a-1cca-580a-876d-f2c664a5da34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345824} {:answer/id #uuid "37a50610-1330-582a-b2b5-974cd1ceb8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345829} {:answer/id #uuid "593dcbaf-fdcd-52ea-8b38-1a3a56714e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346117} {:answer/id #uuid "f61dabae-a838-5c18-bd37-715b475a71a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347534} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc05612-e5a3-549f-8dbf-07cc6363573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37348c9a-1cca-580a-876d-f2c664a5da34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345824} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b9401c-4707-577e-a1c6-56f80e0c7ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3b65f93-463c-54d1-85ca-57a9749888f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-7905" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n- $AE=EA={I}_{3}$\n- $B={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AE=EA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -10 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb0a517-2a62-5ad4-abb7-32db925dcbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396171} {:answer/id #uuid "378c3d9c-f434-5a36-b4c6-e35a124290f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396172} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb69d4-cf84-531e-a891-dec63188d912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396173} {:answer/id #uuid "e1648b74-cbef-5884-9e5c-c49ee87da068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396174} {:answer/id #uuid "94c1155a-b3ff-59dd-b24f-e9f04aac265d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c1155a-b3ff-59dd-b24f-e9f04aac265d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396175} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5359f3-29e9-58eb-b41e-ef40c0bf51eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17761662-c8e1-5053-be1d-f17ca1632a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7915" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $P$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(P)=4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(P)=0,$ we conclude that $P$ is not invertible. Since $P$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $P$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $Q$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Q)=0,$ we conclude that $Q$ is not invertible. Since $Q$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $Q$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $R$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $Q$ are singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f4c8c5-a1e0-50d2-90e3-c842b69b61a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345916} {:answer/id #uuid "8be8bc87-925d-5a80-b272-9812c2eb7b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346563} {:answer/id #uuid "b024a727-8b31-5d76-a59f-dbb3e082e615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346889} {:answer/id #uuid "fac11c48-254b-548d-81fb-bf48b11a35c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347566} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7ffdeb-b39d-53fd-a335-3c0a492a7c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f4c8c5-a1e0-50d2-90e3-c842b69b61a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345916} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4d4e4d-7b06-5451-ae6d-dff63e644b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2b98504-334c-5f63-b626-52b1babf6c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-79170" :question/problem "![](images/14/1407fa82fd00d07311cb34c6a3d5f0d0c79808d321d049d74114a6f4f2e5f6a6.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad4bfcf-ac83-5c47-928b-5b1a7fc0f104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183674} {:answer/id #uuid "df3e3991-8f50-5de8-b75b-5960efe17e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183675} {:answer/id #uuid "f2572169-df3d-5c99-975a-9f3f678ee3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183676} {:answer/id #uuid "f28c8896-b9fe-5342-9cba-cebeee74006f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183677} {:answer/id #uuid "095c622d-5db2-5a48-942e-b312abb3f7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183678}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d980f41-06e1-5562-995c-dd718d6a8c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081bfb29-fde1-5481-b123-8eac743f086f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399099} {:answer/id #uuid "51e08aac-9339-503c-9a66-b74268d4d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399100} {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ce8598-f6eb-5e70-b2e5-0d95b7a5f429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399102} {:answer/id #uuid "6704d952-af2f-5f8a-a722-c90281d08d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} :answer-field/id #uuid "1848e56b-8e02-510d-9f75-0f28e4f3c082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92d6b12-34ec-5e74-a8fa-e75755bbf55a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79188" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f11f8f5-14af-5418-a30f-2cdb361b260a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346029} {:answer/id #uuid "60d87398-f4f2-5513-9687-a427c1be6407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346190} {:answer/id #uuid "6661a258-7ca8-5569-93ca-fc1dce78c2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346245} {:answer/id #uuid "80d564fe-11e4-5e17-ae8d-364ca396dbfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346476} {:answer/id #uuid "d68fa614-0fdb-5265-9177-7d915486a0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d87398-f4f2-5513-9687-a427c1be6407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346190} :answer-field/id #uuid "00426326-692f-5b6d-a52b-4521d86868ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c8948ed-478d-597d-8d84-3303f297f4dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79192" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2f63f3-85a4-55bf-a395-74537aff84ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345587} {:answer/id #uuid "35590547-774e-5b38-9b3b-c11f13078f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345812} {:answer/id #uuid "97a231cb-9741-5f6d-bdb9-9a1deed0940a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346679} {:answer/id #uuid "c93fa12b-ab82-59d4-918f-7a96fbadc4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347104} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf5f9e-fccf-5706-aeb7-33fe7e70deb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35590547-774e-5b38-9b3b-c11f13078f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345812} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c2e10e-3b72-5e83-a5e0-5d16c4723896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97d013bd-90fe-54ca-b491-25e886bc0e3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f632187-9689-5dcb-8607-35b8892e2f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345770} {:answer/id #uuid "482f9eb4-7bcd-514b-8d01-90bf4b1be5b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bd68d-398a-5f81-9d3b-2d620308a60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346702} {:answer/id #uuid "d8491bfd-8d7f-547e-bf7c-f3bb48bb9729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347256} {:answer/id #uuid "f907922f-5150-5e85-afd6-29d01f5304a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482f9eb4-7bcd-514b-8d01-90bf4b1be5b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a244fb-0550-5775-8987-a65a296a9de2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06e8a49e-e560-5c26-89ae-564e1f5a8b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79294" :question/problem "![](images/40/407f89e40691ddaefd912c97dc9e8b15a08e0120ad2cf1c9a277417cd8380e98.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(7,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(7,5),$ we substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-4)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 16 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =16-4 \\\\ y & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004712b6-f610-5c1b-85fc-ea7f5dac42b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a-2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345356} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9c9fb7-186e-5b20-a5ae-9968ec5bb0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(a+1)}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345629} {:answer/id #uuid "5f647f49-d7f6-51ed-a318-7ae05732cfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(a+2)}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346174} {:answer/id #uuid "6edfa3c8-26af-59e1-83da-3da4f171bbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a+2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346319} {:answer/id #uuid "8714236f-9c2b-58c3-a954-b1f5b9039774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-3a}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6edfa3c8-26af-59e1-83da-3da4f171bbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a+2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346319} :answer-field/id #uuid "c86f26bc-cd60-5415-a7c8-50d3d722dac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "381e5b72-d586-538b-b748-3282ad434825" :question/math-academy-id "q-79545" :question/problem "Write $3+\\frac{2}{a}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{a}{a}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a+2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17060fa7-3524-5310-9dfd-dc07b4e8832c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345555} {:answer/id #uuid "8e08b27c-99f7-56ac-8b9f-96e0b8ceef2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346586} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae7946-dd0b-5491-a675-76007cd01c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+k}{k+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346842} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dcd984-a6b9-52d2-bddf-c9136d14c8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t-2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce330a49-fcee-5fd6-95ef-e1da5c0f2f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{k+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e08b27c-99f7-56ac-8b9f-96e0b8ceef2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7650f596-e2e4-5bc7-be67-53414e53752a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e830cbad-9670-547b-94f1-5280434ab395" :question/math-academy-id "q-79551" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{k}{k+3t}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2(k+3t)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{k}{k+3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{k}{k+3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{2k}{2(k+3t)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218cb034-3e85-59b7-a903-56bfc598b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345650} {:answer/id #uuid "5f26a8c0-2af0-5bee-a1cd-0bf2f5569905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346172} {:answer/id #uuid "6db0d065-e8ef-5454-9cf2-b8d92a429fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346309} {:answer/id #uuid "afcb4450-4ae6-54b7-b7e5-2a45c0d057a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346883} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fffe14-90a9-51ed-b231-0b2a4af2d1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8fffe14-90a9-51ed-b231-0b2a4af2d1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347102} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7510d2-2b7e-5e14-bd9b-6604c31ce57e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de44d5ab-abd2-5362-b1b7-cef838348808" :question/math-academy-id "q-80107" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1f31073c260d8111cca3d5de1ec6db3e0f7f76a13c9a41a71b5512acd17f704.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above can be the graph of the polynomial $y=-{x}^{5}+4{x}^{2}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=-{x}^{5}+4{x}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=5)$ is odd and the leading coefficient $({a}_{5}=-1)$ is negative. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $D$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17600165-efc3-5882-9c7a-cb635534d60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345557} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba85996-663d-5ad4-b02a-6d0bddc40c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345591} {:answer/id #uuid "37b2b4a7-6276-57d8-81aa-bd4be528f582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345830} {:answer/id #uuid "92b2d726-2dc6-525f-b4d0-e8875bc3f2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346633} {:answer/id #uuid "d529acba-b4d3-55d8-bdf2-155c9910a474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ba85996-663d-5ad4-b02a-6d0bddc40c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345591} :answer-field/id #uuid "f579af6d-d006-5c83-962e-5ae07618e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bbd6a2b-6a4a-5f26-8d86-72b22a64837d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80135" :question/problem "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77dc6fdd-2db7-55bf-abb5-b59442a1464c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346402} {:answer/id #uuid "b477c45c-b737-549b-91b5-d1091460ac32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346923} {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} {:answer/id #uuid "e341ede8-046b-52c2-b8b6-aa4cf9fe8ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347350} {:answer/id #uuid "fe903f8c-2fae-5ab6-9e3a-9928ee1b850e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df7bc06-d4ba-5a57-ab49-fe4b9ffa350a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b478a4e0-e373-5d4c-8f17-c97eaae0c7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80136" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159c485c-001f-5031-b26e-f83f2bfeaf3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345538} {:answer/id #uuid "8be5f15a-55d3-55ce-9ad8-ff6f91eee7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346562} {:answer/id #uuid "936ec69f-b82c-51aa-b0f0-19d6eaccfe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346640} {:answer/id #uuid "940a0cb2-9a0f-54ac-81e8-736d1cba6508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346643} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9d53ec-b344-5b9f-8c8e-a292d0704445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8be5f15a-55d3-55ce-9ad8-ff6f91eee7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346562} :answer-field/id #uuid "419a1eeb-4e9d-5a0c-8435-a16640782af5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9f0ee2e-ecf1-5ebf-8f05-484d1b3df712" :question/math-academy-id "q-80138" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02915c82-8065-5e74-82a1-9495fe20c4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.854)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345371} {:answer/id #uuid "053a825a-5e0a-5eee-97c6-c642f5c2abaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345397} {:answer/id #uuid "89addc54-f82c-5eac-8aae-245bc3c57ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.916)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346545} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7af4b8-beb1-58ef-8b7a-b1c7ae4dca86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.054)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346571} {:answer/id #uuid "90a16488-0e85-5bd0-9ee2-b77c876e71b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.156)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "053a825a-5e0a-5eee-97c6-c642f5c2abaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345397} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38fa9b7-ea07-551f-b484-bf242ae05284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6b79f0a-30fd-5a34-a89c-78169fdf9be8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80147" :question/problem "$2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)≈$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.955\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =8+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =24 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e73b8bcf-79c2-5326-b396-d7d3e06badaa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f9ea4b3-6eb3-5082-8914-c38bee676387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346600} {:answer/id #uuid "a81dd2b8-40a6-5066-9db4-1ab9177b5325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346814} {:answer/id #uuid "d900a914-eda6-5172-8d8d-8f38cd423706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347264} {:answer/id #uuid "dd36e61a-1034-5c73-9d7e-707ff278bb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347291} {:answer/id #uuid "de5af851-d906-5d9f-a62b-f9c3421ec820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f9ea4b3-6eb3-5082-8914-c38bee676387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346600} :answer-field/id #uuid "142b7324-aef0-53ea-9091-db8282ddff85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68c48374-0bdd-59bd-ad8a-c986ebcde36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8027" :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b75e759-23b9-5a00-8a2f-7c0ca516d97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345736} {:answer/id #uuid "910cdafc-67fc-587c-87f0-bdba48190064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346616} {:answer/id #uuid "b75eed07-3b22-5842-aac1-37ec3c1762bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346950} {:answer/id #uuid "c106e615-06ca-5d4a-9046-a19b20c78b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347019} {:answer/id #uuid "d48896a5-f655-5550-962f-afc68a851fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910cdafc-67fc-587c-87f0-bdba48190064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346616} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b44f15-bb40-5519-8a0c-c8353afc8e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0f13e6-ba4b-56eb-b269-f07bb6ae2732" :question/math-academy-id "q-80303" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a7b19b50822235a2dcbfcdf74984735a1e26b2b775180b24bdc2bcbf24c588b.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n- $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433015c8-b02a-5d3b-9bc4-7270edca0b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345929} {:answer/id #uuid "444cadbf-3703-59f5-bad4-46404cc1a695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(x+1){(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345940} {:answer/id #uuid "654ada61-08a2-5d34-998b-5c8db9d07e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbb7820-ea22-5b52-910b-269aa2f0dbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346310} {:answer/id #uuid "f282e323-500b-5dcf-a254-2d713e281555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654ada61-08a2-5d34-998b-5c8db9d07e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5873ca-3084-51bf-9b1d-3de807002492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad55c8cb-7248-5482-9f28-0afc365f8908" :question/math-academy-id "q-80348" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b086759a25af7e464c0c8be78d36cd4d8458c5c25e7c84cb6ecb28b2d59e9df.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b68fad9-861a-5a5d-b8d5-ff1e2fa15ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{7}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346430} {:answer/id #uuid "8724b35c-787a-52ef-80ff-eaf99f6a667a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{5}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346523} {:answer/id #uuid "94213796-5be7-57ae-81b2-17ed395cf18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346645} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ec07fb-d5b8-585e-82ea-e6c5d87d6e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}(x+2)^{5}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346910} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c1df98-76d9-50de-b003-8111fdce77bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}(x+2)(x-1)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94213796-5be7-57ae-81b2-17ed395cf18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346645} :answer-field/id #uuid "db472ae2-2f21-533d-93c0-3a75b3b10cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0154a31e-007f-558f-af24-913ca9813ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80352" :question/problem "![](images/19/1956e8c684cc03538877d3ec5d1e72d3d3f48b63341f7739b9a18962b080f1e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=1,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d37a5d-f38e-5e9f-b4c2-8557a961d8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345385} {:answer/id #uuid "21e41ad5-4ee1-5fe7-b639-bc9b977dc4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345653} {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} {:answer/id #uuid "a3665dd9-b15a-5d76-8f3c-92d873804b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346766} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ae151a-4d90-50cb-ad3c-f2ecfc64dcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4.989\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} :answer-field/id #uuid "e647b151-d969-5d70-9827-bf43ae96b615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a752e22-d98b-55fa-871d-a83c493259aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-8076" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{5})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{5})| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125a7499-daea-5b5a-bef6-ea8655c63411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345509} {:answer/id #uuid "12b079a4-9cf6-57d5-b910-bde76a8fe294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345512} {:answer/id #uuid "447bc679-763d-5ae0-afdc-316c890e6827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345942} {:answer/id #uuid "92dc2963-35f2-5f74-93b5-e7c999f47bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346636} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ff8f7c-6d0e-56a7-ba0f-b776d57b7c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92dc2963-35f2-5f74-93b5-e7c999f47bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346636} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c00a6f3-3bb4-556d-900d-ebc65c30a86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c929adfb-38d7-5819-8554-7aa009ddd6f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8078" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=3\\sqrt{3}+3\\text{i}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=6$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9be02ade-3dd2-5ec3-a532-78d1151dc89f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number Shown on an Argand Diagram in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd8fff4-79c1-5e30-9ce6-337b2a20b872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\,\\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349292} {:answer/id #uuid "79b311bf-65c7-58fc-b381-6bee661fc901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349569} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecd9ca2-c07f-5373-8dc5-c59dfe7aa36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349609} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9d3205-d44e-57ec-95f3-fb33fbb6e446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.678\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349963} {:answer/id #uuid "f769ac7b-dfab-556e-af9c-c783920eee8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.678\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79b311bf-65c7-58fc-b381-6bee661fc901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349569} :answer-field/id #uuid "10320074-e8c3-5c1b-828c-3f9b61cfea74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92d2189e-bd4e-5b46-a533-1fb76600704b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8082" :question/problem "![](images/18/18dd1668d0e9eaeb1be7919821707bbf1d472b22b00acf68a921a3522b2f5f7f.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4-2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-2}{-4})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈\\pi +0.463\\,64… \\\\  & ≈3.605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =3.605$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9849b9-397b-5fb9-bb4b-01f3d013c5c2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5878e4-f118-5e48-9f1f-25b06f4e11bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/4672e5a5e616c8c6f4d40d1f29a2a8794da4e8d61c8bab09b7483ec292a91410.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394087} {:answer/id #uuid "785d13cd-3fdd-5848-9cdb-db6e373bbb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f5bf56cb4e4546079d89fd97b9533676116d4cff5435cbc926b925af5d3f637.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394088} {:answer/id #uuid "9770d4f6-dde0-5f7a-aeb2-5671a5981656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/54496f08a7d4d6b5d37713ffe6d4bc903d48da90def0b273c114fb3b27e32bc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394089} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec43ec3-b120-5bf6-bbac-590a300de61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394090} {:answer/id #uuid "027e492d-1183-5ffe-8e93-74878e08032d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb590309256b4836583132f707ceb405472d104908f5df45a0d41dfa18c84a30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec43ec3-b120-5bf6-bbac-590a300de61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394090} :answer-field/id #uuid "18abe65c-6c5d-58b2-9bd6-68ded8dd9d89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "620f4a44-3dbc-5f00-8f93-44670447e38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8093" :question/problem "![](images/36/369dde2e95b52356b71972b8f1103430e0a840d3d0478626dc5a78cba0f51a41.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $ABCD$ is shown above. Which of the following is ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′},$ the image of $ABCD$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 8 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-1,3),(0,6),(0,8),(-1,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38cdd4-2914-5fbf-af5a-f896fca82487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348534} {:answer/id #uuid "46388f94-0b9a-5819-926f-46042d3247f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349085} {:answer/id #uuid "46e780ae-e427-5db3-9e7b-87fd979da562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349091} {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f16d9b-438b-5abd-801a-9c54b0392c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} :answer-field/id #uuid "07393c80-442e-58d3-986a-670d473a0e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebfe6ff0-a2a2-5246-ac85-4546ec479cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cf5972-f40e-5217-8db4-af4d714d0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348511} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff5e665-aaa0-5a32-9ee9-f1bfeace33e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349171} {:answer/id #uuid "86623a7d-5813-5d35-94af-16d6fb07a0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349673} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ecf398-d445-5f07-9e39-0e0872bbfc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350178} {:answer/id #uuid "e061e2c5-0620-5228-ad52-cedd9b116361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86623a7d-5813-5d35-94af-16d6fb07a0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349673} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a944731-6928-5c95-9932-5dd54d8c5f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f524ac1c-6637-5a2f-a68c-7f9c181d3393" :question/math-academy-id "q-81297" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{5x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8d2777-c68c-5bac-b02b-e76ea7832227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348585} {:answer/id #uuid "4440720a-4b25-56ac-acfb-e35454aaf42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349068} {:answer/id #uuid "aa03c041-3060-57a7-9832-43fc7a4091d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349958} {:answer/id #uuid "b607c09a-ead1-5f9f-8366-b91cefea5b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350056} {:answer/id #uuid "fe48bdf6-69b4-58e6-ab4b-56b33ca29990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa03c041-3060-57a7-9832-43fc7a4091d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349958} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ab50f6-ff51-5cc2-a2e4-1ac35c1df983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69d362d8-5140-5de9-97ea-ddf053c6a1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81306" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-7}{kx-4}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{5})\\cup (\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e75273e-f9fe-5904-9b97-df326138bc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348572} {:answer/id #uuid "56bde1c1-ec8e-5414-abd3-cf3a35ed829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349240} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec2a49-c0cb-50b3-96d0-78c9ed276005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349486} {:answer/id #uuid "710ec1a0-ca77-5baf-9aea-64447273a88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-6)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349487} {:answer/id #uuid "76603cce-9fe2-582e-98c8-b6b5dfe79841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,6)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ec2a49-c0cb-50b3-96d0-78c9ed276005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349486} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d01f1a9-302b-5e9b-a9b7-f275dfb86ddd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6996c90-1b1f-5226-b63d-1aa92068c6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81416" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-6}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6b1316dd9c7f2a40c23de08dde63cbc6402593c6209d99384279a7c1ae29da6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808a85c2-ca4a-542f-8886-908783750d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349626} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd3458d-3fcd-5615-89ef-119fa3a75163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349718} {:answer/id #uuid "99b11afc-eea4-51cc-90b6-2bcc44f1ccd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349822} {:answer/id #uuid "ec838bdb-4a1f-5b1a-b168-73687add9f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350513} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d92b5e-622a-5e98-8d37-c85026582415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd3458d-3fcd-5615-89ef-119fa3a75163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349718} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fff443-d103-5115-9f6d-56a670c1f10e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dc88ce5-1862-53d4-934e-e04fef66ce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-81532" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a08b123-1007-51d0-9626-6957cea1cbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348819} {:answer/id #uuid "57b185da-3c33-5e53-b896-4c58bbe65ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349253} {:answer/id #uuid "65cf0fb8-b80b-5dd5-873d-133d51e5ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349378} {:answer/id #uuid "d17b82e0-699f-5810-bbfd-d8426a2e29ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350286} {:answer/id #uuid "e029609d-c698-570a-9c3e-6c5b9a4f3d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b185da-3c33-5e53-b896-4c58bbe65ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349253} :answer-field/id #uuid "8380bcb3-1b34-51e5-86b8-2bf85dedf899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0891f6b-b8f3-59bf-bdab-23a45deeee45" :question/math-academy-id "q-81536" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\pi +2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ee5a37-5407-5f2c-b35d-818c7b51de99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348485} {:answer/id #uuid "a96ee961-091c-5bc7-94e9-4fc2414a6608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349955} {:answer/id #uuid "c965a8db-cf91-5534-9a2e-b2b2d81d2a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350220} {:answer/id #uuid "f934958a-918e-544c-9c79-ee9005be989f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350636} {:answer/id #uuid "ff59dfe4-69fc-5f06-a333-183188f57b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96ee961-091c-5bc7-94e9-4fc2414a6608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349955} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9569062-abe3-5fbf-aea5-b41339436a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "483db40e-99c9-5ee4-bf30-82e490a0ea46" :question/math-academy-id "q-81542" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdbc89b-3531-5aca-8ba9-f97488020ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348987} {:answer/id #uuid "4113848c-c05d-566b-b1dc-5db84a4d0393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349038} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a3fae-b9d5-5b5e-bd0f-dcd4106b4c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349305} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb5e873-76c9-58bf-8d7a-7e75030cf3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349581} {:answer/id #uuid "abfe1148-d014-58cf-9667-7f0d1e092c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a3fae-b9d5-5b5e-bd0f-dcd4106b4c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6083aa-ab96-52a6-8f56-aa3dc31b367b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57be2016-4f15-558e-8c73-c2bdbd52fe97" :question/math-academy-id "q-81546" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{6},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3e445b-242f-5183-8134-e1a2873f586c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348570} {:answer/id #uuid "29c5d455-ea88-583b-878a-5b2da6e2eca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348815} {:answer/id #uuid "87848d38-9a7f-500f-8ee0-d03c8430c2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349681} {:answer/id #uuid "c66561f4-bc83-5fd5-927f-843045ce31e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350193} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d22d26-f36a-5676-9a92-d9439050a127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c5d455-ea88-583b-878a-5b2da6e2eca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348815} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d872cb-4071-5344-a1bc-bda53e5bcc36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7bdacc5-30cf-5786-b5bc-164c930bb79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81567" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $4$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le -1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le 3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $4$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 3,\\,f(x)\\ge 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3beb2bf3-85a6-5351-8707-3d22b1ee479b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348988} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcaac38-7ca5-5639-8065-5ed95845a2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349583} {:answer/id #uuid "b9239d6e-0398-536e-96ea-926946d19cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350087} {:answer/id #uuid "f19cb4c0-bc6d-5293-af8d-6cb78b330c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350559} {:answer/id #uuid "f665cb8d-c8b2-5193-ac92-977971e1d72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f665cb8d-c8b2-5193-ac92-977971e1d72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350603} :answer-field/id #uuid "718ade24-34b7-5766-a493-7153cbbe8e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6209583e-a305-5e36-8803-ab5525f3111a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81568" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -2,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e37e352-2ebd-519b-b752-f0f303d5c735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2d114b-f236-58ac-bb08-c56f9ad411f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349151} {:answer/id #uuid "76d9e37c-df77-5f7d-b7e9-be9174f15aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349539} {:answer/id #uuid "88cba209-3291-583a-9d5b-5e1a16336323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349689} {:answer/id #uuid "c32d5354-3148-51a9-aede-6809d8386c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e37e352-2ebd-519b-b752-f0f303d5c735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4a398d-6bb6-535f-9a7e-aa4d0e416252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c63c20b6-af06-5453-a6bf-493adf10c2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81581" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x+{30}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx+{30}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{30}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcbf67f7-3f48-58b0-b313-1ddf0a908258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b66fd6bb3e9b37fd3a72927a1fb4df87eaffea3bf5579e681fee3aeda4885e75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394272} {:answer/id #uuid "8d294a24-e87b-5b66-a362-f26102dab0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa78e1ab38afbc0cd7bf92061d176a816c7a8a0e1d08c8825486c45dbe8fb5f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394273} {:answer/id #uuid "4b69504f-e290-5883-87fb-ed2e3d29bb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/39168e3de9d42254e0c1528ab7ed40f2a4c0e88e9570705b773e3875d5057df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394274} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa84fc9-3d0a-59df-b5c7-bacc5b53fb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c95c1f93ccc6c6c2ff7264ac5bca90680ff2c3c745d95acb0f81d288bf4e498a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394275} {:answer/id #uuid "fb14ca5e-3e3c-5a83-b18e-eead2e86a390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d64f49a072fd7841d1d1b81e396673f3addcfa05005128e969dbb6ea4697d358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b69504f-e290-5883-87fb-ed2e3d29bb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/39168e3de9d42254e0c1528ab7ed40f2a4c0e88e9570705b773e3875d5057df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394274} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eaae499-eee4-5eed-af75-d74268bec6c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c0ad4a3-22f9-57a4-8323-31593f95cf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-81823" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d473b8ea-8d04-5ece-95ea-65e737aef3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394286} {:answer/id #uuid "03ae5c24-7a66-5265-969a-3f12d946ddaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/979157d993a86617fabf4b2dfacc6d4c1d973885b42be081b4bcffb36058e091.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394287} {:answer/id #uuid "47c3f52d-cad6-592b-8617-cf6147446ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4bf42e3bdeb61feccd68cf4eeb9e3c4a40e8b903a5739df77c993e9aa4abc993.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394288} {:answer/id #uuid "d69be06f-7ba5-5d06-aad7-b3459d303317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf449d44237c85963bf4fbb134da290b39aac4ebd0b128313052cbd1970db67f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394289} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef8d4dc-0422-509e-9d94-1b349ccfac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5b/5b4684361e541532d1be24de046f17201811146fbb0413222c5100e5a3eb80dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d473b8ea-8d04-5ece-95ea-65e737aef3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394286} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5bbd5a5-fd75-5bef-9814-1007ac35605e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7364bd3c-e82a-5e8d-b8cd-06a6c72405af" :question/math-academy-id "q-81831" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7f75505591eb8bf407e63b4255ae65548bde65921f6f00cca9031191ddc0905.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/77/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a3ec35954243105f9bb6c9f1f2cc21c02358f90855f9f36f0a590092eb93d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b7885b-ae95-575e-97f6-8a71e1b57ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404193} {:answer/id #uuid "65627c21-66cb-5f02-8ba6-0c37010c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404194} {:answer/id #uuid "dea138f5-3204-5f95-9262-ff365ea22b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404195} {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404196} {:answer/id #uuid "21d62bc4-f68d-55ab-b0ca-9dee218d1167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404196} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bfa454-0c70-5d48-80bc-d4490bd1867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b338b5cf-801c-50c4-9622-d9ab0fa4da90" :question/math-academy-id "q-81863" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379491ec-d15c-5213-ab37-21be3454aa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348948} {:answer/id #uuid "5675de99-d640-5a79-8625-1bf0d30b0cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349237} {:answer/id #uuid "a4caf121-6c14-50f0-aeb3-fd2aed32744f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349918} {:answer/id #uuid "f28f2ddb-3aaf-5065-ac5a-85f3f483c5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350574} {:answer/id #uuid "ff18f0bb-e463-5951-b0c4-3badeb9977f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5675de99-d640-5a79-8625-1bf0d30b0cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349237} :answer-field/id #uuid "6545ad2b-c92a-5487-bc20-82c927dbe614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e53bfbce-8b32-5854-943b-ffd612dcb153" :question/math-academy-id "q-81994" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)+6.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =0 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb2860c1-8ed6-5c0c-8f43-c1ba75225c02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Number With Positive Imaginary Part"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0922c4b6-d25d-551a-9cb3-5200b81e3ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348522} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6579a7-a15a-511f-8185-1f0d53d6b68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{1}{3+5\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349605} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce7bbc3-fac1-53f3-a1de-86a5cc73fcd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349845} {:answer/id #uuid "b477f944-a4de-5199-ad13-36fc4346d627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350042} {:answer/id #uuid "b5672995-afd4-5eed-ad77-cd6033ca9a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5672995-afd4-5eed-ad77-cd6033ca9a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350052} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1cebfd-d980-591f-bac4-9d01fdc80d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be49ea4-410e-5bb3-9aa7-a4c55fff790b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8220" :question/problem "What is the complex conjugate of $z=3+5\\text{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $5\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-5\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7895-83a7-5868-921c-57c50450ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348975} {:answer/id #uuid "69962bbe-75b8-5ae4-99aa-4f7910f71965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349425} {:answer/id #uuid "7df6bfdf-a463-5e56-a7b0-8cc61b55f17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349601} {:answer/id #uuid "b60fe42a-6aaa-59ac-b35e-8ac4d19e26ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0ed589-e9f7-57d6-8078-aa521972d708" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61e93f3c-1103-58d6-a5e4-4e5649a9f688" :question/math-academy-id "q-8258" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-2+5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} {:answer/id #uuid "305d1949-c98f-5233-bbfc-3a89cbfc0c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.723)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.723))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348876} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d4a2e-e6b9-5883-a2d2-4f5ef0dac7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{15}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.486)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.486))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349428} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3107c4-9eb8-5fe0-9fae-1d7e36aa0717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{13}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349728} {:answer/id #uuid "b1280a6d-c454-5f65-93e2-6c3b8cbf0d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.641)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.641))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e03d1a2-30fd-5e5d-a7b1-322a0564c17b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d310c8c-3e11-5226-89ae-78e76ac29fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-8263" :question/problem "Express $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5+12\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865035a9-dfe6-51bf-a5dc-624240267541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087156} {:answer/id #uuid "981d3229-34da-5547-9622-76e7a57b9b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087157} {:answer/id #uuid "ff08f834-c93c-58b6-a6eb-be4ab302b1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087158} {:answer/id #uuid "5826ab39-99cb-5f0d-a91a-c8e47d4e7431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087159} {:answer/id #uuid "58fc334c-401e-58ca-8084-7bbee1c81411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "212166f2-0485-5ec6-aaa6-538998150651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} {:answer/id #uuid "9017ed40-b00d-55a3-877f-c3d4b075da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396199} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a1da3a-abde-5c78-94f6-9beb1ca8472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396200} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2fc306-e79b-5a44-8145-c511e597d027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396201} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5eb027-48d1-5f9c-9af9-618b997023bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} :answer-field/id #uuid "169533db-26aa-5a83-a7a7-fa42593fd137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a96319-3bb3-5dd4-a3ac-27f5effab07d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8331" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}ky \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} {:answer/id #uuid "354f131e-ae5c-51ab-8348-a87034b594f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348929} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24dbbe-b87e-5b97-894f-05bae0099c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349162} {:answer/id #uuid "75e8d951-cd2e-5911-8618-a190ffa28f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349532} {:answer/id #uuid "faa13be6-1fba-5e10-bc32-18634ce1a08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f262d7-769f-525a-a77b-2e82355e8aef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8324b04-6c39-5b50-ae14-3bae7af8be74" :question/math-academy-id "q-83840" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336d5b0b-46fb-592f-8727-3a06de946703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352042} {:answer/id #uuid "82209c5f-513d-558a-80b5-81bcad912708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699} {:answer/id #uuid "da005a29-2e30-58a3-8eda-87359ad503f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353459} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb5462e-f1c5-5814-83c4-35e425cabf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353512} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b25f7-d0b9-528a-83fb-6c5aa038ad8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82209c5f-513d-558a-80b5-81bcad912708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f23fd7e-f0b2-5902-a559-b924fdec7db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40af28c7-87f4-525e-8058-edd168d8bbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84045" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =0 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-5 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f49b77-3f70-5736-a4c7-a05bb6ed569d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352168} {:answer/id #uuid "554aaa63-ee3f-5b2c-990b-cdf71234977d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352322} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f7faf-4c3f-5afe-9d56-a3b4cb8058b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353043} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e7e4b4-817f-5394-970d-8f4fb1759ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353087} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ed0f1-7eff-5e0a-8239-9514ffae76bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f7faf-4c3f-5afe-9d56-a3b4cb8058b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353043} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada8a35c-66a8-54fb-a5e9-7567b26d684a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc19725-defa-5a63-87f9-f8a8fdd626b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-84108" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22276dc2-a2b9-5cbc-8c67-2986ce0007da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351876} {:answer/id #uuid "3406dbe4-4a00-5248-9893-ce53b859d49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352051} {:answer/id #uuid "75d0455a-51e7-593c-ac60-e49ce930f87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352603} {:answer/id #uuid "b754e390-db22-57b2-ae0d-9a1ac89fa09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353135} {:answer/id #uuid "d21008dd-c70e-516f-b57d-efa1aabd8110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b754e390-db22-57b2-ae0d-9a1ac89fa09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353135} :answer-field/id #uuid "b989a899-d8b9-5376-915b-28b5573472a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f063fbc-7799-5914-b601-73bb4ae22301" :question/math-academy-id "q-8431" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/07/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1b0500-415f-5429-89d9-fbbb92a98ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352546} {:answer/id #uuid "cd29b07a-70bf-5347-a5b0-93e51c59301b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353348} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a586c-680f-580d-a120-8ca0ede7f5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353548} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8e504f-d2c6-5f83-8ce5-d298b778f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353653} {:answer/id #uuid "fde2ebba-1cad-5211-b6e3-3a258bce156d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde2ebba-1cad-5211-b6e3-3a258bce156d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353793} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee08007-7eba-5dcc-a203-e575bd3f6bdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eede2a0-b32f-5395-87d1-df0fef1f0013" :question/math-academy-id "q-8454" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=7-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e83c96f-a2c1-5ee2-824c-2bc3c4684376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352543} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee7573c-36ff-53f4-99b6-0a96510e5c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352927} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5b458a-6221-5d09-9efd-8161ba724cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353210} {:answer/id #uuid "c0edaaa2-2945-5a5b-be55-284981757f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353233} {:answer/id #uuid "f25e0d44-17a1-56d9-867b-896fe9d214d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e83c96f-a2c1-5ee2-824c-2bc3c4684376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352543} :answer-field/id #uuid "db5c6a1e-bc94-579d-a193-eb175ccc8696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4205fd8-bc08-5712-a15e-589056630f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8477" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36227757-8ddf-553b-8b32-b91ec5d6cc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352069} {:answer/id #uuid "c03243fb-e94b-59f6-86f6-290c965d292d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353221} {:answer/id #uuid "c20895a7-2bb5-5227-a177-0ecbb7cd7ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353245} {:answer/id #uuid "da22af31-accb-56a4-9e9c-447aa29ffd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353462} {:answer/id #uuid "ef32583d-a162-59dc-8096-c688f7e6b4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20895a7-2bb5-5227-a177-0ecbb7cd7ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353245} :answer-field/id #uuid "800d8a79-f760-51db-a6cd-5f43e77a1247" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4803d779-141e-515c-910c-0b6c763e47ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-84947" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc451fa80b0a65a817c66822571f434736c1c5c0992b64de3c5998931bba75fd.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/70/7032a13b6672d8bf66ead31a54577d25bbd47640d8c3cffeedd069d57eec9338.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2aa742-ff1a-54b2-97a0-0bebb3cecd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351696} {:answer/id #uuid "1e476e2b-6825-5337-9a24-4ffc405d5de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351836} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf837f8-b43f-5397-b414-cc6a493481ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352388} {:answer/id #uuid "abe94b80-ee11-5325-9ca6-efb5e6a9e1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353025} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b8605f-99d6-5803-9576-5d4c433892db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe94b80-ee11-5325-9ca6-efb5e6a9e1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353025} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea64b02-1605-5ead-bad0-1b95b5a94b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3b2857-f742-54ff-a16a-524aeaa787ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-84952" :question/problem "![](images/83/83217df9245edba7b09f5b29edd8862564c3c4e772ba2da45e1c4e11f10c7747.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a left-opening parabola is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80c3336af95cf45198f4d8cf3b8718c7dcd2512f4720948d0edda3d987a6aa0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} {:answer/id #uuid "967290dc-71b3-501d-8605-16ee088f7c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352870} {:answer/id #uuid "a4389b55-ae83-53e1-9f8d-a4182b6742a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352968} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bc970-f051-5dfb-a7f1-db97122d6816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353237} {:answer/id #uuid "ded0b80c-41d3-53a3-9730-fea5f6f67367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c729ab-9ef7-56a2-b67a-e30b58f4144d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a3d9a2c-1761-5baf-9e5d-4958b26130d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84954" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=5y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260ee64b-60f9-577f-b9cd-899af296d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351917} {:answer/id #uuid "73b8b006-61f8-5c7a-906f-a83360295df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352580} {:answer/id #uuid "b91e1eea-f224-503d-85e7-b2faa41fc99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353149} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9be550-efdd-5dc5-80d3-353296eb9f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353327} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b644a-cfec-5d4f-922b-5a3e24daabe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260ee64b-60f9-577f-b9cd-899af296d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351917} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcee7007-39c0-575f-87b4-92eaeeb2b54a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b3fff3-7ca8-5a05-bacf-2186c0ce6253" :question/math-academy-id "q-84956" :question/problem "A pizzeria has nine different toppings from which to choose. How many different pizzas can be made using only three toppings?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ toppings, so $n=9.$ We want to select $3$ of those toppings for each of the pizzas, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{504}{6} \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1755d2b2-8ef2-5841-967a-37231874078e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351776} {:answer/id #uuid "22aa46f6-d9f6-52a0-921d-e699a6fdabc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351880} {:answer/id #uuid "aefddd11-ffea-5b50-af77-6441d3546955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353052} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ef2388-0f72-5871-b0be-8276cb5fe915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353453} {:answer/id #uuid "debdfcd2-ba10-5c33-a152-32714bf2926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debdfcd2-ba10-5c33-a152-32714bf2926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353500} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb52f3bb-59b4-5a00-86e6-1550bcc92127" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e1ceb1-ad30-5708-98e7-b7ac9be19ae7" :question/math-academy-id "q-84959" :question/problem "Sara has a box of $9$ colored pencils. In how many ways can Sara pick a set of $4$ different pencils from the box?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ pencils in the box, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those pencils, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of four pencils is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,024}{24} \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e930c14-7407-53e8-ac84-31d7b250449f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352544} {:answer/id #uuid "b52289e0-c35b-55ea-bb6c-8d9b85ecdd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353114} {:answer/id #uuid "be45fc23-d089-5e1e-8141-82c146916a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353192} {:answer/id #uuid "d674dad4-60ae-5a18-b33c-d0228e2d1e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353424} {:answer/id #uuid "e1026acb-201d-5cef-afeb-3adf4233a361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b52289e0-c35b-55ea-bb6c-8d9b85ecdd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353114} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc1d491-e28d-5249-b404-4f835405e6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3962390-93bb-5eee-a524-e14f0426b20f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84964" :question/problem "Given any two points in a plane, there is a unique line that passes through both points. Given six points in a plane, what is the maximum number of unique  lines that can be made using these points?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ points, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those points for each of the lines, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of unique lines is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a079a-3b5d-5568-a29d-6f2901c3f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352210} {:answer/id #uuid "66130b54-d958-5449-b32e-e09731f751e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352466} {:answer/id #uuid "72d2ceff-58e4-5cc6-ae01-b9e7dcc9c275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352571} {:answer/id #uuid "75807951-bdad-56c4-9ac3-85b96c3b3874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} {:answer/id #uuid "bf82ba33-b385-51d9-aff0-7b68257c69a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75807951-bdad-56c4-9ac3-85b96c3b3874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} :answer-field/id #uuid "46991009-d0a6-523f-9684-9c96c88c491a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2eb1bd6-8295-533c-aa8b-65d0d7da5480" :question/math-academy-id "q-84967" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+3)^{2}=5y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(y-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-3$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e302810-8b14-5c8c-8bbb-245386a66b7c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30951a52-0069-56b4-9e82-2949d8f2a2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352014} {:answer/id #uuid "398fdaaf-9bcb-5cc4-a059-9e1bd02851bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352098} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad09f62-dd43-5478-8c92-4ff635da99d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352255} {:answer/id #uuid "a86861b4-432a-5da2-8768-af22fdb06c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352997} {:answer/id #uuid "daf7b837-7463-559a-9f9e-1ebc8d42ec4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86861b4-432a-5da2-8768-af22fdb06c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352997} :answer-field/id #uuid "9374288c-3b96-5e55-b494-e802520d3be4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a083623-223f-5e1c-9de8-75212036d214" :question/math-academy-id "q-84975" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-8x?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(-2)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-2.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290f943b-3622-50d0-924f-31daf0acc563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351948} {:answer/id #uuid "77ef416e-a3b0-5473-bf35-8e1898c31847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352617} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5e835c-ce12-5b70-842e-5afce9455637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352642} {:answer/id #uuid "82456399-e756-555d-81e7-d2b347aafff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352701} {:answer/id #uuid "f32745fa-711d-5685-96d5-140a1218db12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82456399-e756-555d-81e7-d2b347aafff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352701} :answer-field/id #uuid "07f7e8b6-11ec-5218-9222-810bc50fc8fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fda58513-e80c-5984-860c-d5cee3ac12ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-84980" :question/problem "How many made-up $3$-letter words can be made using only $5$ vowels, where each vowel cannot appear in the word more than once?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $5$ vowels, so $n=5.$ We want to select $3$ of those vowels to make-up a $3$-letter word, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different made-up words is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaed84db-333e-5211-84c5-2d24c1d90316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394622} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1b16c-2ec4-5950-92f8-59f867978e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} {:answer/id #uuid "542940cc-ac17-5702-9c22-d185b7bb47c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394624} {:answer/id #uuid "2e70f827-6004-57e0-825e-f0e45fc164d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394625} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a0e84-7492-5601-b7a4-02b8e70ea90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1b16c-2ec4-5950-92f8-59f867978e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d9fa78-c6ea-5ff0-a6cb-0b7ec2cbcfc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ef67e9d-56e9-502f-9f38-dd41f4d01e7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84983" :question/problem "A bag contains $9$ different colored marbles. Jamie takes $4$ marbles from the bag, one at a time, and puts them in the order they were taken from the bag to create a sequence of colors. How many different sequences of colors are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ colored marbles, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those marbles to make a sequence of colors, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose the marbles is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3\\,024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9ef577-0c58-5250-8f48-0bbbe35c8e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351846} {:answer/id #uuid "231e02be-dea0-583f-843e-fcb4e5fa89ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351883} {:answer/id #uuid "38f8babc-447a-5c84-a823-e46c61910574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352093} {:answer/id #uuid "42e860bc-bb64-5cbf-89e8-43d07d97b308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352179} {:answer/id #uuid "fa439ab6-a80b-5522-ab03-1b73a0a6baab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa439ab6-a80b-5522-ab03-1b73a0a6baab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353758} :answer-field/id #uuid "41e7848d-fd2b-5ccc-b4f4-baab707c46a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57957360-6f31-5e05-8a45-38b0c037b34e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84994" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{12}{3})$ means the same thing as ${}_{12}{C}_{3}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fa39e5-ce50-500d-851f-de9eb3b12eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394629} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e55eef-a2a6-5dcc-8dbd-442ffb6f3b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394630} {:answer/id #uuid "24b6f013-8ec7-5811-963d-26ce3311756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394631} {:answer/id #uuid "cde0fe8a-36a8-5e30-8de3-aa6250e1a52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394632} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dca18c-0cda-53f2-ae56-98a74295c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dca18c-0cda-53f2-ae56-98a74295c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394633} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee128c64-8462-52f2-aa05-6a02c9deb300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccb665c6-b272-5591-95cb-e38be54fe76e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85044" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{20}{16})$ means the same thing as ${}_{20}{C}_{16}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and $r=16,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{C}_{16} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-16)!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{4!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{4!\\cancel{16!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{116\\,280}{24} \\\\  & =4\\,845.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcef49e9-f6e2-5903-88c5-5668ea726fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394636} {:answer/id #uuid "00becd7c-07c7-51a1-8d01-c22ee90258d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394637} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbc2ffb-e921-5ab1-a79b-de22cae70506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394638} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f8eb3e-2322-5192-b908-a3996052fac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,304" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394639} {:answer/id #uuid "043e4ba5-d6b6-52c1-acd4-3439e275df92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcef49e9-f6e2-5903-88c5-5668ea726fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394636} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c1eba9-fa9c-5dcd-af76-d9dc0545ee45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6727864-63ba-5449-a9b7-0dc7ad66d6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85046" :question/problem "${}_{52}{P}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=52$ and $r=2$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{52}{P}_{2} & =\\frac{52!}{(52-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{52!}{50!} \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\cdot 51\\cdot \\cancel{50!}}{\\cancel{50!}} \\\\  & =52\\cdot 51 \\\\  & =2\\,652.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9972d5bf-226c-5e87-988b-2fa3e0b4fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394643} {:answer/id #uuid "82c57328-7703-57d6-912b-2ecb7333260d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394644} {:answer/id #uuid "57d5dc3c-cc5e-57df-b81a-64cfa2b4c5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394645} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc1825b-3805-54c0-b2fd-b20b91c26d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394646} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2571b5-b785-5912-b9bf-d0e4a3fa1a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c57328-7703-57d6-912b-2ecb7333260d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394644} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff3b3c1d-8754-5e1f-a814-29025fd4b1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1198cf2-96b8-5b7c-bdc4-b79e3231eb29" :question/math-academy-id "q-85049" :question/problem "$P(7,7)=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(7,7)$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{P}_{7}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7,7) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =5\\,040.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80a4eee7-a763-5c28-ae3c-5bce78222c87" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Area Scale Factor of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0173ef52-efe0-5287-8dcc-837391e54372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351576} {:answer/id #uuid "2642ddef-2ffa-5c85-8740-8bde5e925aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351920} {:answer/id #uuid "5c33000c-ac5a-51fc-90f5-02db3e52de35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352382} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9b953a-f463-5966-8e99-453d5b1a9ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352384} {:answer/id #uuid "e4701335-ff8c-583f-872d-f3bebae93b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c33000c-ac5a-51fc-90f5-02db3e52de35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352382} :answer-field/id #uuid "39338680-aa39-58da-b22d-dcf7ddf94bb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6f8c2df-477d-5e6f-b732-724c19c5001f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85069" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|8\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-2)| \\\\  & =|24+10| \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092218 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040aa995-2a95-5978-ae2e-5bf3e64df54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351604} {:answer/id #uuid "327eef0b-6d33-56b7-b7fa-65647e6064b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352034} {:answer/id #uuid "8813249a-4b08-5911-ab6e-9dfd026fab0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352759} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf46c88-e2c1-5c74-915f-85de76f4b18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} :answer-field/id #uuid "496d734d-db4b-581d-ac63-001e27aa3e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ab581aa-df37-556d-9193-036f42c477b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8516" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${150}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta ={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d832f7-9f3c-5a0e-881e-07763aca8c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351767} {:answer/id #uuid "28bc318f-788c-5840-ac67-f1d2f1710e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351946} {:answer/id #uuid "47de3a50-2a65-5111-9ffd-7c98bb5a5fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352229} {:answer/id #uuid "89a48c39-b8c8-590e-9f09-c7b3c91c777f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352767} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5e597-9dc4-58a0-8f3b-f94c2d23f1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28bc318f-788c-5840-ac67-f1d2f1710e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351946} :answer-field/id #uuid "19dbdeac-7821-534b-91fc-f462d8368066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "031552db-aefc-5369-b9be-0f86727e7b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-8550" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{5\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}]}
